{{!-- The homepage Surface's body, in the Layout it is handed.

`layout` is `@custom.homepage_layout` from home.hbs, or a fixed value from a Layout override
template (ADR 0006). This partial holds the one {{#match}} on it, so a template decides where the
value comes from and nothing else.

WHY THE PAGINATION IS HERE AND NOT IN THE LAYOUT. {{pagination}} reads its numbers off the
current context, and the {{#get}} in home/window.hbs renders its block with the query's own
response as the context — a control inside it would offer nine pages of twelve on a Surface that
has six pages of eighteen. Called here it reads the page's own pagination. (`@root` would rebind
it in place, and gscan rejects `@root` as an unknown global, so the structure carries it
instead.)

`pagination.pages` guards the call because the helper throws outside a paginated context: with
the layout in docs/routes.example.yaml this Surface IS page 1 of the `/` collection and pages
through it, but a publication that hands `/` to a `route:` has no pagination at all, and one
that has fewer posts than a page has no second page to offer. The editorial slots are indifferent
to all three, which is what the {{#get}} in home/window.hbs buys.

A2 narrows its article column to 1120px and leaves the blocks below it — the subscribe band and
the writers strip — at page width. The override is a wrapper here because it has to contain the
pagination too: `--container-max` is what every `container` reads, and the control belongs to the
grid above it rather than to the page. --}}
	{{#match layout "Editorial masthead"}}
		<div style="--container-max:1120px">
			{{> "home/window" wide=true}}

			{{#if pagination.pages}}
				{{pagination pt="pt-14"}}
			{{/if}}
		</div>
	{{else}}
		{{> "home/window"}}

		{{#if pagination.pages}}
			{{pagination pt="pt-14"}}
		{{/if}}
	{{/match}}

	{{> "newsletter" anchor="newsletter" pt="pt-16"}}

	{{> "writers" anchor="authors" headingId="au-head"}}
